                  <provenance type="#e"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_189">Durham. Benedictine
                        cathedral priory of St Cuthbert</orgName><p><q>Liber wⁱ Cuthbert ex dono
                           domini <persName role="fmo">Willelmi Dalton</persName>
                     monachi</q></p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners"><persName role="fmo">Sir Thomas
                        Phillipps</persName>, MS 23867; his sale, Sotheby's, 21–26 March 1895, lot
                     136 to Quaritch (£25), who sold it to Harrassowitz. Messrs Quaritch in 1961,
                     who sold it to E. Rossignol, bookseller, Paris. This was the last known
                     location reported in both MLGB2 (1964), p. 34 and the Supplement (1987), p. 76,
                     at which point the book was described as untraced.</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Now in the Staatsbibliothek zu Berlin, with
                     this location first noted in MLGB3 (2009-2024). Gerard Achten reports that the
                     Königliche Bibliothek (now the Staatsbibliothek) purchased the manuscript
                     through Harrassowitz at the 1895 Sotheby's auction (acquisition number
                     1894,556). He does not mention either Quaritch or Rossignol in his discussion
                     of provenance. See <title>Die Theologischen Lateinischen Handschriften in
                        quarto der Staatsbibliothek Preussischer Kulturbesitz Berlin, Teil 2, MS
                        theol. Lat. Qu. 267-378</title> (1984), p. 66.</provenance>
               </history>
